<p class=3D"paragraph larva // lrv-u-line-height-copy  lrv-a-font-body-l   =
">
	The Stanford prison experiment, a prison simulation that took place over t=
wo weeks in 1971, forever shifted the field of psychology and its interpret=
ation of human nature. Now, a new <a href=3D"https://www.rollingstone.com/t=
/docuseries/" id=3D"auto-tag_docuseries" data-tag=3D"docuseries">docuseries=
</a> by <a href=3D"https://www.rollingstone.com/t/national-geographic/" id=
=3D"auto-tag_national-geographic" data-tag=3D"national-geographic">National=
 Geographic</a> delves into the study and features firsthand accounts from =
the original =E2=80=9Cguards=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Cprisoners,=E2=80=9D many=
 speaking on camera for the first time.</p>



<p class=3D"paragraph larva // lrv-u-line-height-copy  lrv-a-font-body-l   =
">
	Directed by Juliette Eisner, <em>The Stanford Prison Experiment: Unlocking=
 the Truth</em> explores a new narrative examining the motives of Dr. Phili=
p Zimbardo, the Stanford University psychology professor who oversaw the ex=
periment and gained worldwide recognition following its conclusion. Zimbard=
o died on Oct. 14. According to a press release, the doc examines =E2=80=9C=
the power of perspective and mythmaking.=E2=80=9D</p>



<p class=3D"paragraph larva // lrv-u-line-height-copy  lrv-a-font-body-l   =
">
	=E2=80=9CIn the 50 years since the Stanford Prison Experiment took place, =
I was surprised to learn that few of the study=E2=80=99s participants had e=
ver spoken about their experiences, and that their stories exposed a web of=
 secrets, inconsistencies, and layers that questioned the experiment=E2=80=
=99s seemingly simple narrative about whether human beings are inherently g=
ood or evil,=E2=80=9D said Eisner in a statement.&nbsp; =E2=80=9CIn light o=
f Dr. Zimbardo=E2=80=99s recent passing, this project couldn=E2=80=99t be m=
ore timely in its exploration of human nature and the repercussions of diff=
ering narratives.=E2=80=9D

